# {{PROJECT_NAME}}

Django 5.2 app sold in tiers: {{TIERS_LABEL}}. Core ({{CORE_TIER_LABEL}}) lives in `core/`; each
higher tier is a Django app in `modules/<tier>/`. A client release contains only the tiers that
client has, because `scripts/build-release.sh` removes the rest from the code.

## Getting started

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
cp .env.example .env
python manage.py migrate
python manage.py createsuperuser
python manage.py runserver
```

## Tiers

- `{{PREFIX_UPPER}}_TIER` in `.env` picks the active tier. Development defaults to `{{TOP_TIER}}`.
- `python manage.py {{PREFIX}}_feature` lists feature switches; add a name and `--on`, `--off` or
  `--reset` to change one. Switches also appear in the admin (django-waffle).
- In templates: `{% load tiers %}`, then `{% can "perm" %}...{% else %}...{% endcan %}` and
  `{% menu "main" %}`. In views: `@permission_required("perm", raise_exception=True)`.
- Core never imports from `modules`. `core/tests/test_arch.py` enforces it.

Run the suite at every tier before committing:

```bash
for tier in {{TIERS_SPACED}}; do {{PREFIX_UPPER}}_TIER=$tier pytest -q || break; done
```

After changing a module's models, run `makemigrations` with the top tier active so every app is
installed: `{{PREFIX_UPPER}}_TIER={{TOP_TIER}} python manage.py makemigrations`.

## Releases

```bash
scripts/build-release.sh <tier> <version>    # e.g. scripts/build-release.sh {{CORE_TIER}} 1.0.0
```

The script writes a fresh one-commit repo and a zip to `../<project>-releases/` and prints the
SHA-256. The client installs `requirements.txt` on their host. Paths that never ship are listed in
`release.exclude`.

## AI agents

`AGENTS.md` has the rules for coding agents, including the commit review gate. A human approves
every commit; `scripts/propose-commit.sh` asks for that approval on the terminal.
